Spilling capture state to disk
==============================

This document describes why and how TShark can keep the dissection state of a
capture file on disk instead of in RAM (the --spill-dir option), what it does
not cover, and what the measured cost is. For the mechanics of the allocator
itself see section 4.1.1 of README.wmem and the doxygen comments in
wsutil/wmem/wmem_backing.h.

1. The problem
--------------

Analysis state grows with the capture, and when it no longer fits in RAM the
process dies. There is no partial result and no way to trade speed for
capacity: g_malloc() fails, GLib aborts, and nothing is produced.

Measured on a 4.1 GB / 9.53 M packet capture of real traffic, with every
malloc-family allocation accounted:

    peak heap, single pass                       9089 MB
    peak heap, two-pass (-2)                    10184 MB

Attributing that by workload (flow-complete subsets of the same traffic, and
synthetic captures that vary one dimension at a time):

    per TCP conversation                     ~4.0   KB
    per packet, TCP sequence analysis        ~138   B
    per packet, frame_data (two-pass only)    112   B
    per packet, DNS-only traffic             ~600   B
    per packet, HTTP-only traffic           ~1400   B
    fixed floor (dissector registration)      ~91   MB

So capacity was roughly RAM/1070 B per packet for a mixed workload. The
existing mitigation, -M, periodically destroys the whole session: it bounds
memory by discarding conversations, reassembly and all other per-capture
state, which changes the results, and it is incompatible with -2.

2. Goal
-------

Make the RAM requirement independent of the capture size, accepting that the
run gets slower, and without changing any output. Specifically:

  - identical results with and without the option, including taps and -2;
  - no state discarded, unlike -M;
  - no per-dissector changes, so the 3000+ dissectors are unaffected.

Out of scope: live capture (state is bounded by the capture duration, not the
file), the Qt GUI (random access needs more than this; see section 8), and
parallel dissection (an orthogonal track).

3. Architecture
---------------

A backing store (wmem_backing_t) is a single large shared mapping of a sparse
temporary file. Allocators hand out regions of it instead of regions of the
heap. Because the whole range is reserved and mapped once, addresses are
stable for the life of the store, so nothing above the allocator - not the
dissectors, not the data structures, not even the allocator's own chunk
logic - has to know where the memory lives. Pages are resident only while in
use; under memory pressure the kernel writes them to the backing file rather
than failing the allocation.

The block allocator took all of its OS memory from exactly three places
(8 MB blocks, jumbo allocations, and their frees), so pointing it at a store
is a policy change, not a new allocator: wmem_allocator_new_backed() passes a
store down to wmem_block_allocator_init_backed(), and everything else about
the allocator is unchanged. With a NULL store the behaviour is exactly as
before.

TShark creates one store from --spill-dir before epan_init() and attaches it
to the allocators whose contents live as long as the capture file:

    file scope            conversations, TCP analysis, per-packet proto data,
                          streaming reassembly, reassembly tables and keys,
                          reassembled buffers
    addr_resolv_scope     name resolution caches
    frame_data_sequence   the per-frame index built by two-pass analysis
    fragment_data_scope   copies of individual fragment payloads

Epan scope is never backed: it holds the dissector registration tables, which
are always hot and are a fixed cost.

4. Lifetime segregation
-----------------------

The last two arenas exist because a block allocator never returns a freed
chunk to the OS - only whole blocks that have become entirely free, and only
on garbage collection. Mixing allocations with different lifetimes in one
arena therefore pins it at the sum of the high-water marks of all of them.

Reassembly is the case where this bites. The fragment payload copies are
dropped as soon as their reassembly completes, while the reassembled output
and the bookkeeping live until the capture is closed. Putting both in file
scope raised total footprint on an HTTP capture from 4804 MB to 8900 MB.
Giving the fragment copies their own arena, collected every 4096 releases,
brought it back to 4754 MB: blocks go empty, garbage collection returns them
to the store, and the store releases the pages (and, where the platform
supports hole punching, the disk blocks).

This is the general rule for anything added later: an arena should hold
objects with one lifetime.

5. What is still on the heap
----------------------------

    dissector registration tables (epan scope)        ~91 MB, fixed
    tap accumulators                                  grows with the number
                                                      of conversations
    GHashTable internals of the reassembly tables     grows with fragments
    wiretap read buffers                              bounded per packet

Measured residual on the 4.1 GB capture with -z conv,tcp: 141 MB, against
8954 MB of state in the store.

6. Correctness
--------------

The option changes where memory comes from, never what is computed, so the
verification is equality of output rather than a new set of expectations:

  - test/captures, 339 real captures: -V -x and -2 -V -x byte-identical with
    and without --spill-dir;
  - 9.53 M packet capture: -z conv,tcp output identical, 149018 rows;
  - -T fields, -T json, -2 -T json, -z endpoints,ip identical;
  - the project test suite (2089 tests) and reassemble_test unchanged;
  - wmem_test runs its full allocator torture suite, including jumbo
    allocations and the invariant checker, against a file-backed allocator
    (/wmem/allocator/blk_backed) and asserts the store is fully reclaimed.

The one behavioural subtlety is teardown order: a tvb wrapping reassembly
data can be freed after file scope is gone, so the free callback checks
wmem_in_scope() first.

7. Cost
-------

Nothing is written while state is under the --spill-after watermark, so a
capture that fits in RAM is unaffected. Past it, the cost is page faults and
writeback on the part that went to disk, because eviction is page-granular
while the access pattern is flow-granular. The same 4.1 GB capture (9.53 M
packets, 8954 MB of state, -z conv,tcp) at different watermarks:

    watermark      in RAM    spilled     wall
    no spilling        -          -       36 s
    16384 MB      8954 MB       0 MB      36 s
     6144 MB      6136 MB    2818 MB      47 s
     2048 MB      2045 MB    6909 MB     431 s
        0 MB           0    8954 MB      698 s

So the penalty tracks how much is on disk, and the watermark is the knob that
trades speed for capacity rather than a switch that makes everything slow.

Flow-sharded parallelism helps more than it looks like it should, because it
shrinks the working set each process touches. Four workers on flow-disjoint
shards of the same capture, each holding ~2.3 GB of state:

    4 workers, no spilling                      11 s
    4 workers, watermark 4096 MB (nothing spills) 11 s
    4 workers, watermark 0 (everything spills)  23 s

23 s against 698 s for one process spilling the same total state: one process
randomly touching a 9 GB arena misses cache constantly, four touching 2.3 GB
arenas do not.

Smaller captures and other shapes, at watermark 0 (worst case):

    capture                            in RAM     spilled    peak heap spilled
    real, 199 k packets                 0.84 s      0.81 s       187 MB
    synthetic 2 M packets, 50 k flows   7.25 s      7.17 s        46 MB
    synthetic 1 M packets, 500 k flows  4.16 s     14.2  s        47 MB

(macOS 27 on APFS, 10 cores, 16 GB.)

The third row is the adversarial case: 500 k flows touched round-robin, so no
flow is ever in cache. MADV_RANDOM is what makes it tolerable - without it
that run took 40.9 s, because readahead multiplied every fault.

Host matters as much as workload. The 9.53 M packet capture at watermark 0,
same code, in a Linux container on ext4 (8 cores, 10 GB):

    real, 9.53 M packets / 4.1 GB      70.8   s    118    s

1.7x rather than 19x. Writeback of randomly dirtied pages, not the spilling
itself, is what differs between the two systems.

8. Limits and next steps
------------------------

Capacity. With the heap term now flat, the limits are disk, the address space
reserved by --spill-reserve (64 GB by default, and currently fatal rather
than extensible when exhausted), tap accumulators for runs that use -z on
very large captures, and 32-bit frame numbers.

Speed. Page-granular eviction against flow-granular access is the remaining
problem. The fix is to make file scope resolve to a per-conversation
sub-arena, so a cold flow's state is page-contiguous and faults back as one
unit; that also makes explicit LRU eviction of idle flows possible, which
would subsume -M without discarding anything that is still reachable.

GUI. The Qt interface is unchanged: it never calls
wmem_set_capture_backing(). Enabling it there is a few lines, but random
access to an arbitrary packet needs dissection state as of that packet, which
needs the per-flow arenas above plus periodic checkpoints.

9. Platform notes
-----------------

Linux releases pages with MADV_DONTNEED and punches the disk blocks out with
fallocate(FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE); macOS uses fcntl(F_PUNCHHOLE). Note that
FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE is declared in <linux/falloc.h>, not <fcntl.h>, and
fallocate() itself needs _GNU_SOURCE - get either wrong and the hole punching
silently compiles out while everything still works, just without returning
disk blocks. Windows has no equivalent of the mapping scheme used here, so
wmem_backing_file_new() fails there and --spill-dir reports the error.

Resident set size is a misleading measure on both platforms: resident pages
of the backing file count towards it even though they are evictable without
swap. On macOS use "peak memory footprint" or the malloc interposer in
test/benchmark; on Linux use a cgroup limit, which is also the clearest
demonstration - under "docker run --memory=1g" the unmodified run is
OOM-killed (SIGKILL) after 3 s having produced nothing, while the same run
with --spill-dir completes with identical output and a resident set bounded
by the limit.

10. Reproducing the measurements
--------------------------------

test/benchmark contains the capture generators, the malloc-budget interposer
and a Dockerfile for the Linux side, including the demonstrations that an
unmodified run dies where a spilled run completes with identical output.
See test/benchmark/README.md.
